This section reviews some basic concepts and definitions involving C++ 
class templates, for reference. These comments focus primarily on class
templates, rather than function templates.

\section developer_template_concepts_defs_sec Class Template Definitions

A C++ class template definition provides a recipe for defining a class, 
rather than a complete class definition. A template definition includes 
one or more template parameters, each of which may represent either the
name of a type or a value of some specified type. 

The syntax for defining a class template starts with the keyword "template"
followed by a list of parameter declarations. As an example, consider the
class template FArray in the Util namespace, which is a template for an
array container of fixed size. This template takes an integer parameter
N that gives the number of elements in the array and a typename parameter
T that gives the type of each element, and is defined using the syntax
\code
   template <int N, typename T>
   class FArray {
      ....
   };
\endcode
where the ellipses indicate the actual class definition, which is not
shown. The symbols N and T are the template parameters. 

\section developer_template_concepts_instantiation_sec Template Instantiation

A concrete class definition is constructed when a compiler **instantiates**
a class template with a particular list of template parameter values. The 
actuql types and values assigned to template parameters when a template is
instantiated are referred to as **template arguments**.  When a class 
template is instantiated with a specific list of arguments, the compiler 
generates object code for the resulting class for the memory layout of the
class and for all of its member functions.

A **specialization** of a class template is a class whose name is given by 
the name of a class template name followed by a valid list of template
arguments in angle brackets. For exanple
\code
  FArray<10, double> 
\endcode
is the name of a specialization of the template FArray with arguments "10" 
and "double".  Each instance of this class is a 10 element array of double 
precision real numbers.  

A specialization of a class template may be created either by instantiating 
the template or by defining an **explicit specialization** that redefines 
or specializes the behavior of the template for a specific list of arguments. 
An **instantiated class template specialization** is a class template 
specializaiton that is created by instantiating a general template, rather
than by explicit explicit specialization.

\section developer_template_concepts_impexp_sec Explicit and Implicit Instantiation

An **explicit instantiation definition** is a type of C++ statement whose 
only purpose is to instruct the compiler to instantiate a specific 
specialization of a template. The syntax for an explicit class template 
instantiation definition starts with the keywords "template class" followed 
by the name of the required template specialization. For example, the statement
\code
   template class FArray<10,double>;
\endcode
is an explicit instantiation definition for the class template specialization
FArray<10,double>.

A class template may be instantiated by either explicit or implicit instantiation.

Explicit instantiation occurs when instantiation of a template is triggered by 
an explicit instantiation definition.

Implicit instantiation occurs when a C++ compiler instantiates a class 
template the first time that it encounters the name of a specialization in 
a context that requires the creation or use of an instance of that class, 
rather than as a result of an explicit instantiation definition.  Implicit 
instantiation of a class template may be triggered by a declaration of a 
variable or function parameter that is an instance of a template 
specialization, or by instantiation of a class that is derived from a 
class template specialization. 

\section developer_template_concepts_declare_sec Explicit Instantiation Declarations

By default, a C++ compiler will immediately attempt to compile a class
template instantiation the first time it is required to construct or use 
an instance of that instantiation during processing of a translation unit
(i.e., the file that is created by the preprocessor by combining a source
file with all of the other files that it directly or indirectly includes
via preprocessor include directives. The object code for the class that
is created by this process can be re-used throughout the remainder of 
the translation unit but, by default, is not accessible to be re-used
in other translation units. 

This default behavior can be altered by the use of  "explicit instantiation 
declaration". This is a language feature that was introduced in the 2011 
update of the C++ language (C++11), which substantially expanded the
possibilities for valid ways of workng with templates in C++. The syntax 
for an explicit instantiation declaration starts with the keyword "extern" 
followed by syntax for a corresponding explicit instantiation definition. 

As an example, consider the class template Basis, which is defined in the
Pscf::Prdc namespace. Like many templates used in PSCF, this is a template
with a single integer parameter, named D, that represents the dimension of
space, for which the definition looks something like this:
\code
   template <int D>
   class Basis 
   {  .... }; 
\endcode
An explicit instantiation definition for template instantiation with
an argument D=3, if it appeared within namespace Pscf::Prdc, would be
\code
   template class Basis<3>;
\endcode
An explicit instantiation declaration for this instantiation would be
given as
\code
   extern template class Basis<3>;
\endcode
Note that the syntax for the explicit instantiation declaration is the
same as that for the explicit instantiation definition, except for the
addition of the keyword "extern" at the beginning of the declaration.

The appearance of an explicit instantiation declaration is an intruction
to the compiler to disable implicit instantation of a specified template
instantiation for the remainder of the relevant translation unit, and to 
instead look for object code for that instantiation during linking.
An explicit instantiation declaration thus instructs the compiler to 
treat a class that is created by instantiation a class template much 
like it would usually treat a non-template class, for which it would
look for object code for the class definition during a separate 
linking process that will occur after compilation. Such a declaration
thus also contains an implied promise to the compiler that a 
corresponding explicit instantiation definition exists somewhere else
that resulting object code will be accessible to the compiler during 
linking.

\section developer_template_concepts_file_sec Template File Organization

When a class template is instantiated, the compiler must have access 
to both the class definition (which contains declarations of interfaces
for all member functions) and the definitions of all class member 
functions. This requirement must be met for either explicit or implicit
instantiation.

To guarantee that this requirement is always met, the standard advice 
in textbooks and online discussions of C++ templates is that definitions 
of member functions for a class template should always be placed either:

  - within the header file that contains the class template definition, 
    or 
  
  - in a separate file that is included near the end of the class header 
    file. 

These options are equivalent from the point of view of the compiler, 
since both guarantee that definitions for all member functions will be 
accessible in any file that includes the class header file.  In older 
versions of C++, prior to C++11, this was indeed the only safe or 
practical way to organize files involving templates.  

The introduction of explicit instantiation  declarations to the language 
in C++11 has, however, made it practical for class member functions for 
some types of class templates to be placed in a separate file that is not 
included by the header file. This usage is discussed in detail on 
the next page.

